= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $Z_t=\langle\kappa_t\rangle$, where the angle brackets average only over walk paths, and use $\mathbb E$ only for the environment. Every nearest-neighbor path of length $t$ has probability $(2d)^{-t}$, so the finite path sum is exactly the walk expectation. It depends only on environment layers through time $t$, and hence is $\mathcal F_t$-measurable. Its factors lie in $[1-\varepsilon,1+\varepsilon]$, so $Z_t$ is positive and integrable at each fixed time.

For any fixed path through time $t+1$, the environment variable $h(t+1,\xi_{t+1})$ is <independent> of $\mathcal F_t$ and has <mean> zero. <Conditional expectation> therefore removes its last factor. Summing over the $2d$ possible last steps of each length-$t$ path cancels the extra factor $2d$ in the path probability. Thus
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[Z_{t+1}\mid\mathcal F_t]=Z_t,\qquad Z_0=1,\qquad \mathbb EZ_t=1.}
$$
This is the <positive random-environment path-weight martingale>. It is nonnegative and bounded in $L^1$, so the <martingale convergence theorem> gives a finite limit $\zeta\ge0$ almost surely. At this stage one can conclude $\mathbb E\zeta\le1$ by <Fatou's lemma>, but not yet equality; the second-moment bound in part (b) will prevent loss of <mean>.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a fixed environment, use two <independent> copies of the walk to express $Z_t^2=\langle\kappa_t^{(1)}\kappa_t^{(2)}\rangle$. The product is finite and nonnegative, so we can interchange the two expectations. At each time $j$, if the walks occupy different sites, the two environment signs are <independent> and their factors have product expectation $1$. If they coincide, both factors use the same sign and
$$
\mathbb E(1+\varepsilon h)^2=1+\varepsilon^2.
$$
Different time layers are <independent> even when a site is visited again. Therefore, with the simultaneous collision count $I_t=\sum_{j=1}^t\mathbf1_{\{\xi_j^{(1)}=\xi_j^{(2)}\}}$,
$$
\boxed{\mathbb EZ_t^2=\left\langle(1+\varepsilon^2)^{I_t}\right\rangle.}
$$
This is the <collision-count second moment for independent path weights>. It involves equal-time intersections, not arbitrary intersections of the two spatial ranges.

The difference walk $D_j=\xi_j^{(1)}-\xi_j^{(2)}$ has <independent> bounded increments symmetric about zero and a full-dimensional step distribution. It may hold at zero for one step; such a hold counts as a positive-time return. The allowed transience fact gives
$$
\rho=\mathbb P_0(D_j=0\text{ for some }j\ge1)<1.
$$
Moreover $\rho>0$ since the two first steps can be equal. Restarting the <independent increments> after every return to zero shows that the total positive-time return count $I_\infty$ has the zero-based <geometric distribution>
$$
\mathbb P(I_\infty=k)=(1-\rho)\rho^k,\quad k=0,1,2,\ldots.
$$
Indeed the probability of at least $k$ returns is $\rho^k$, and subtracting successive tails gives this law. Consequently, whenever $\rho(1+\varepsilon^2)<1$,
$$
\sup_t\mathbb EZ_t^2\le\left\langle(1+\varepsilon^2)^{I_\infty}\right\rangle
=\sum_{k=0}^\infty(1-\rho)[\rho(1+\varepsilon^2)]^k
=\frac{1-\rho}{1-\rho(1+\varepsilon^2)}<\infty.
$$
A sufficient small-noise condition is $0<\varepsilon<\min(1,\sqrt{(1-\rho)/\rho})$.

The <L2 martingale convergence theorem> now gives $Z_t\to\zeta$ in $L^2$ as well as almost surely. One can see the <mean> preservation directly: for $t\ge s$, the <martingale> identity gives $\mathbb E(Z_t-Z_s)^2=\mathbb EZ_t^2-\mathbb EZ_s^2$. The <second moments> are increasing and bounded, so the sequence is Cauchy in $L^2$ and its limit is the already identified almost-sure limit. Thus
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E\zeta=\lim_t\mathbb EZ_t=1.}
$$

= c
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For each fixed $m\ge0$ and $T\ge m$, remove the first $m$ weight factors but retain the same walk law:
$$
Z_T^{(m)}=\left\langle\prod_{j=m+1}^T(1+\varepsilon h(j,\xi_j))\right\rangle.
$$
This quantity is measurable with respect to $\mathcal G_{m+1}=\sigma(h(j,x):j\ge m+1,x\in\mathbb Z^d)$, since the averaging over all walk positions is deterministic and involves no earlier environment variables. The bounds on each deleted factor give, pointwise for every environment,
$$
(1-\varepsilon)^mZ_T^{(m)}\le Z_T\le(1+\varepsilon)^mZ_T^{(m)}.
$$
Both constants are finite and strictly positive. Taking lower limits gives the exact identity of events
$$
\{\liminf_TZ_T=0\}=\{\liminf_TZ_T^{(m)}=0\}\in\mathcal G_{m+1}.
$$
There is no exceptional-set issue in this comparison. Choose the version of $\zeta$ to equal $\liminf_TZ_T$ when this is finite and to equal $1$ otherwise; it agrees with the almost-sure <martingale> limit, and its zero event is precisely the left-hand event above. Since the identity holds for every $m$, $\{\zeta=0\}$ belongs to the printed <tail sigma-algebra> $\bigcap_{n\ge1}\mathcal G_n$. This proves the <tail event for a vanishing positive path-weight limit> without pretending that the whole value of $\zeta$ is tail-measurable.

The <independent> time layers of the environment are <independent> random vectors, so the <Kolmogorov zero-one law> applies to their <tail sigma-algebra>. It yields $\mathbb P(\zeta=0)\in\{0,1\}$. Under the small-noise condition from part (b), $\mathbb E\zeta=1$ and $\zeta\ge0$, so that probability cannot be $1$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(\zeta=0)=0,\qquad \zeta>0\text{ almost surely}.}
$$
The tail-event argument itself works for every $0<\varepsilon<1$; the deduction of positivity here uses the mean-one conclusion supplied by part (b).
